                                              Links in linux

--> A link is just another name/path that points to the same data on disk.
--> A link count in linux shows how many names points to the same file.
--> link count of a file tells the total number of links a file has.

--> Inode: (Index Node)
----> it is a data structure in linux which stores information about files.
eg: inode number: it is uniquly existing number for all the files in linux.
file permission, owner, file size, data location etc
---->**it does not store file's name or its actual contents**



--> type of link 
1. Hard Link 
2. Soft Link 

1. Hard Link :
--> These links have actual file contents.
syntax:  ln original_file  link_name
eg: ln demo.txt hardlink

--> hard link file is assigned the same inode value as the original file cz they refer the
 same physical location.
--> removing link just reduces the link count , but does not affect other links or original file.
--> deleting the original file will not affect other links.

2. soft link :
--> soft link contains the parh of original file.
--> inode no is difffent from original number
--> deletting the original file, the softlink becomes "dangling" link which points to the non-existing file.




